@page
@model IndexModel

<div class="app-container">

    @* ── Hero Header ──────────────────────────────────────────────────── *@
    <header class="hero">
        <p class="eyebrow">Week 4 Project Demo &mdash; Database Interactions</p>
        <h1>Employee Management System</h1>
        <p class="hero-name">Sherika Fayson &nbsp;|&nbsp; SDC 320</p>
    </header>

    @* ── Welcome / Instructions ───────────────────────────────────────── *@
    <section class="card">
        <h2>Welcome &mdash; Phase 4</h2>
        <p>
            Welcome to the <strong>Employee Management System</strong> &mdash; Phase 4.
            This phase extends the Phase 3 application to add <strong>SQLite database integration</strong>,
            demonstrating persistent data storage and all four CRUD operations:
        </p>
        <ul class="instruction-list">
            <li>
                <strong>Create</strong> &mdash;
                Click <em>Add New Employee</em> on the Employee Database page to insert a new record
                into the SQLite database. The <code>EmployeeDatabase.AddEmployee()</code> method
                generates a new ID and runs a parameterized <code>INSERT</code> statement.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Read</strong> &mdash;
                The <em>Employee Directory</em> section below and the <em>/Employees</em> page both
                load data from SQLite using <code>SELECT</code>. A search bar on the /Employees page
                supports filtering by name, department, or job title.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Update</strong> &mdash;
                Click <em>Edit</em> next to any employee to open a pre-populated form.
                Submitting the form runs a parameterized <code>UPDATE</code> statement that
                overwrites all editable columns for that employee ID.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Delete</strong> &mdash;
                Click <em>Delete</em> next to any employee (with a confirmation prompt) to
                permanently remove their record via a <code>DELETE WHERE EmployeeId = ?</code> statement.
            </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
            The Phase 3 OOP concepts (abstraction, constructors, access specifiers, polymorphism)
            are preserved below — the database layer itself demonstrates them at the data tier.
            Scroll down to explore, or jump directly to database management:
        </p>
        <div class="db-nav-row">
            <a class="btn-primary" href="/Employees">&#128197; Manage Employee Database (CRUD)</a>
            <a class="btn-secondary" href="/Employees/Create">&#43; Add New Employee</a>
        </div>
    </section>

    @* ── Phase 4: Live Database Snapshot ─────────────────────────────────── *@
    <section class="card">
        <h2>Phase 4 &mdash; Live SQLite Database <span class="poly-tag">@Model.DbEmployeeCount record(s)</span></h2>
        <p class="section-note">
            This table is populated by <code>EmployeeDatabase.GetAllEmployees()</code> —
            a <code>SELECT * FROM Employees ORDER BY LastName</code> query against the live SQLite file.
            Changes made on the <a href="/Employees">/Employees</a> page are immediately reflected here.
        </p>
        <table>
            <thead>
                <tr>
                    <th>ID</th>
                    <th>Name</th>
                    <th>Type</th>
                    <th>Job Title</th>
                    <th>Department</th>
                    <th>Pay Rate</th>
                    <th>Email</th>
                </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
                @foreach (var employee in Model.Employees)
                {
                    <tr>
                        <td>@employee.EmployeeId</td>
                        <td>@employee.FirstName @employee.LastName</td>
                        <td>
                            <span class="badge badge-@employee.GetEmployeeType().ToLower()">@employee.GetEmployeeType()</span>
                        </td>
                        <td>@employee.JobTitle</td>
                        <td>@employee.Department</td>
                        <td class="pay-summary">@employee.GetPayInformation()</td>
                        <td>@employee.Email</td>
                    </tr>
                }
            </tbody>
        </table>
        <div style="margin-top:14px;">
            <a class="btn-primary" href="/Employees">Manage Employees (Add / Edit / Delete)</a>
        </div>
    </section>

    @* ── Phase 3 — Abstract Classes Summary ──────────────────────────────── *@
    <section class="card">
        <h2>Abstraction <span class="poly-tag">Phase 3 Demo</span></h2>
        <p class="section-note">
            Two abstract classes demonstrate how abstraction is applied across the system:
        </p>
        <table>
            <thead>
                <tr>
                    <th>Abstract Class</th>
                    <th>Abstract Method(s)</th>
                    <th>Concrete Subclasses</th>
                    <th>Why Abstract?</th>
                </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr>
                    <td><code>Employee</code></td>
                    <td>
                        <code>GetEmployeeType()</code><br />
                        <code>GetPayInformation()</code><br />
                        <code>GetDetailedSummary()</code>
                    </td>
                    <td>
                        <span class="badge badge-hourly">HourlyEmployee</span><br />
                        <span class="badge badge-salaried">SalariedEmployee</span><br />
                        <span class="badge badge-commission">CommissionEmployee</span>
                    </td>
                    <td>A &ldquo;generic employee&rdquo; has no real-world meaning.
                        Every employee has a pay type; the base class cannot define a
                        meaningful default.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td><code>ReportGenerator</code></td>
                    <td><code>GenerateReport()</code></td>
                    <td>
                        <code>EmployeeDirectoryReport</code><br />
                        <code>PayrollSummaryReport</code>
                    </td>
                    <td>A &ldquo;generic report&rdquo; has no content. Header/footer
                        logic is shared; only the body differs per report type.</td>
                </tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </section>

    @* ── Phase 3 — Generated Reports ─────────────────────────────────────── *@
    <section class="card">
        <h2>Generated Reports <span class="poly-tag">Abstract ReportGenerator Demo</span></h2>
        <p class="section-note">
            Both reports below were produced by calling <code>GenerateReport()</code> through an
            abstract <code>ReportGenerator</code> reference. The page model does not know or
            care which concrete subclass is used &mdash; only that the object honors the abstract contract.
            <code>GetDetailedSummary()</code> inside the directory report is also called through
            abstract <code>Employee</code> references (polymorphism within abstraction).
        </p>

        <h3>Employee Directory Report</h3>
        <pre class="report-output">@Model.DirectoryReportText</pre>

        <h3 style="margin-top:1.5rem;">Payroll Summary Report</h3>
        <p class="section-note" style="font-size:0.8rem;">
            Tax Year (set by static constructor on <code>PayrollResult</code>): <strong>@EmployeeManagementSystem.Models.PayrollResult.TaxYear</strong>
        </p>
        <pre class="report-output">@Model.PayrollReportText</pre>
    </section>

    @* ── Phase 3 — Constructor Demo (New Hire Intake) ─────────────────────── *@
    <section class="card">
        <h2>New Hire Intake Queue <span class="poly-tag">Multiple Constructors Demo</span></h2>
        <p class="section-note">
            These three employees were created using the <strong>simplified 4-parameter constructor</strong>
            (employee ID, first name, last name, pay rate/salary/commission rate). Department, job title,
            address, and contact information default to <em>Pending</em> until HR onboarding is complete.
            Constructor chaining via <code>:this()</code> routes the simplified call through the full
            parameterized constructor, keeping initialization logic in one place.
        </p>
        <table>
            <thead>
                <tr>
                    <th>ID</th>
                    <th>Name</th>
                    <th>Type</th>
                    <th>Pay Rate</th>
                    <th>Department</th>
                    <th>Summary (via abstract GetDetailedSummary)</th>
                </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
                @foreach (var emp in Model.NewHires)
                {
                    <tr>
                        <td>@emp.EmployeeId</td>
                        <td>@emp.FirstName @emp.LastName</td>
                        <td>
                            <span class="badge badge-@emp.GetEmployeeType().ToLower()">@emp.GetEmployeeType()</span>
                        </td>
                        <td>@emp.GetPayInformation()</td>
                        <td>@emp.Department</td>
                        <td class="pay-summary">@emp.GetDetailedSummary()</td>
                    </tr>
                }
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </section>

    @* ── Payroll Processing Demo (Phase 2, preserved) ─────────────────────── *@
    <section class="card">
        <h2>Payroll Processing <span class="poly-tag">Polymorphism Demo</span></h2>
        <p class="section-note">
            Produced by <code>PayrollProcessor.ProcessPayroll(@Model.HoursThisPeriod hrs)</code>.
            Each pay amount was computed by calling <code>CalculatePay()</code> through an
            <code>IPayable</code> interface reference &mdash; the same call, three different results.
            <strong>Phase 3:</strong> Each <code>PayrollResult</code> is now created via a
            parameterized constructor (private-set properties enforce immutability).
        </p>
        <table>
            <thead>
                <tr>
                    <th>ID</th>
                    <th>Name</th>
                    <th>Type</th>
                    <th>Pay Structure</th>
                    <th>Period Pay (@Model.HoursThisPeriod hrs)</th>
                </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
                @foreach (var result in Model.PayrollResults)
                {
                    <tr>
                        <td>@result.EmployeeId</td>
                        <td>@result.EmployeeName</td>
                        <td>
                            <span class="badge badge-@result.EmployeeType.ToLower()">@result.EmployeeType</span>
                        </td>
                        <td class="pay-summary">@result.PaySummary</td>
                        <td class="pay-amount">@result.PayAmount.ToString("C")</td>
                    </tr>
                }
            </tbody>
            <tfoot>
                <tr class="total-row">
                    <td colspan="4"><strong>Total Gross Payroll This Period</strong></td>
                    <td class="pay-amount"><strong>@Model.TotalPayroll.ToString("C")</strong></td>
                </tr>
            </tfoot>
        </table>
    </section>

    @* ── Phase 3 OOP concepts are demonstrated above; the database section was moved to the top. *@

</div>

@* ── Phase 3 CSS additions ─────────────────────────────────────────────── *@
<style>
    .report-output {
        background: #1e1e2e;
        color: #cdd6f4;
        font-family: 'Courier New', Courier, monospace;
        font-size: 0.78rem;
        line-height: 1.6;
        padding: 1.25rem 1.5rem;
        border-radius: 8px;
        overflow-x: auto;
        white-space: pre;
        border: 1px solid #45475a;
        margin-top: 0.75rem;
    }
</style>
